    id: "4c21e6dc-896b-49c5-a849-ce19383f324f",
    slug: "doctor-funk",
    name: "Doctor Funk",
    description: "Created by Don Beach in the early 1950s at his Don The Beachcomber restaurant in Palm Springs. Beach was inspired by and adapted a medicinal absinthe-laced limeade, concocted and prescribed decades earlier by Doctor Bernard Funk, a German physician in Samoa. Dr. Funk was the personal physician of Robert Louis Stevenson, the famous author of Treasure Island , during his final years in Samoa. Stevenson, who suffered from lifelong bronchial issues, settled in Samoa in 1890 at an estate he named Vailima. The good doctor reportedly created a medicinal tonic for the ailing writer made with absinthe, lemon juice, lime juice, grenadine, and soda. The potion is described by Frederick O'Brien in his 1921 book Mystic Isles of the South Seas . Dr. Funk arrived in Samoa in 1880 and left to ret",
